<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Alcalá</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1499">1499</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Complutense University of Alcalá</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of Alcalá was founded at Alcalá de Henares, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, by Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jimenez-de-cisneros">Francisco Jiménez de Cisneros</a> under a papal bull of 1499 and opened in 1508. A centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanism and theology, it produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/complutensian-polyglot-bible">Complutensian Polyglot Bible</a>. It moved to Madrid in 1836, becoming the Complutense University, and a new University of Alcalá was founded in the town in 1977.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-alcala.jpg" alt="University of Alcalá" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raimundo Pastor · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Universidad_de_Alcal%C3%A1,_escudo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>On 20 May 1293 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iv-of-castile">Sancho IV of Castile</a> granted Gonzalo Pérez Gudiel, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, a licence to create a Studium Generale, as university studies were then called across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, in Alcalá de Henares. It was to have the same freedoms for teachers and students as the General Study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a>. These studies were modest but survived, and on 17 July 1459 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> granted a bull, requested by Archbishop Alfonso Carrillo de Acuña, for three chairs of arts and grammar at the study of Alcalá. Cardinal Cisneros later integrated these chairs into his new university.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1499 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jimenez-de-cisneros">Cardinal Cisneros</a> founded a university at Alcalá de Henares. Historians call it by several names, among them the Complutense University and the Cisneriana University, and together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-salamanca">University of Salamanca</a> it reached a pre-eminent place among Castilian universities during the Golden Age. Alumni and teachers over the centuries included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-nebrija">Antonio de Nebrija</a>, who held a chair of poetry and grammar and is best known for his grammar of Castilian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignatius-of-loyola">Ignatius of Loyola</a>, who studied theology and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> there, Tirso de Molina, and the humanist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-gines-de-sepulveda">Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda</a>. Pedro Calderón de la Barca studied logic and rhetoric there, and Andrés Manuel del Río, who discovered compounds of vanadium, studied at the university too. It later entered a period of decline.</p><p>By a royal order of 29 October 1836 Queen Regent Maria Christina ordered the university moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. There it took the name Literary University and, in 1851, Central University of Madrid, until the name Complutense was restored in the 1970s. The buildings in Alcalá passed between various businesses for years. In 1975 the Complutense University opened a branch in Alcalá, and in 1977 the university was founded anew as the University of Alcalá de Henares, a name shortened to University of Alcalá in 1996.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Complutense University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> regards itself as continuous with the university Cisneros founded in 1499, while the present University of Alcalá, founded in 1977, uses the historic buildings in the city centre that the older university once occupied. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> named the site a World Heritage site in 1998. The university keeps its traditional humanities faculties and promotes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-language">Spanish language</a> through its teaching and through the Cervantes Prize, which the King and Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> award each year in the sixteenth-century Paraninfo, the Great Hall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Alcalá” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The universities of Salamanca and <b>Alcalá</b>, where Cardinal Francisco Jiménez de Cisneros sponsored the Complutensian Polyglot Bible, nurtured the School of Salamanca, whose theologians such as Francisco de Vitoria debated the rights of conquered peoples and helped lay the foundations of international law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
